---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
const canonical = 'https://mindgracencr.in/aasha/echolalia';
const headExtra = `<title>Echolalia in Children: Meaning, Examples and Support | Aasha</title><meta name="description" content="Learn what immediate and delayed echolalia can sound like, how to respond, and when to ask a speech and language therapist for help."><link rel="canonical" href="${canonical}"><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta name="blog-date" content="2026-11-08"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/min/seo-pages.min.css">`;
---
<Layout headExtra={headExtra}>
  <main id="main-content" class="seo-page">
    <Breadcrumbs canonical={canonical} items={[{name:'Home', href:'/'}, {name:'Aasha', href:'/aasha'}, {name:'Learning library', href:'/aasha/learning'}, {name:'Echolalia'}]} />
    <section class="seo-hero">
      <p class="seo-kicker">Aasha family guide · Speech and language</p>
      <h1>Echolalia in children</h1>
      <p class="seo-answer">Echolalia is the repetition of words or phrases a person has heard. It can happen immediately or after a delay. A repeated phrase may help a child communicate, participate or process what they heard. Look at the situation and the child's other communication before deciding how to respond.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="seo-section">
      <h2>What do immediate and delayed echolalia sound like?</h2>
      <p>Immediate echolalia happens close to the original words. You might ask, “Do you want water?” and hear “Want water?” in reply. Look at what happens next. Does your child reach for the cup, push it away, wait for you to continue or repeat the words while looking elsewhere? Those details help you decide whether to offer water, wait or make the choice clearer.</p>
      <p>Delayed echolalia uses language heard earlier. A child may repeat a line from a familiar story while standing near the door or while playing with a toy. You may recognize the original line without knowing what it means in the new situation. Observe whether the phrase returns at a predictable time and whether the child shows what they want with movement, gesture, expression or a communication device.</p>
      <p>A child may repeat the whole phrase or change some words. Tone can carry information too. A phrase that sounds playful during a game may sound strained during a transition. Treat your interpretation as a possibility. When you are unsure, offer an accessible way for the child to show you what they mean.</p>
      <h2>Does echolalia mean a child is autistic?</h2>
      <p>Echolalia can occur in autistic children. A repeated phrase by itself cannot establish an autism diagnosis. An assessment looks at development and communication across settings, including how the child understands others, participates in play, expresses preferences and copes with different demands. Share what is easy for the child as well as what concerns you.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.asha.org/Practice-Portal/Clinical-Topics/Autism/">ASHA autism practice guide</a> discusses echolalia as one feature clinicians may encounter and considers communication across different forms. For local information about choosing an initial visit, see <a href="/aasha/autism-early-intervention">autism early intervention in Greater Noida</a>.</p>
      <p>Aasha is located at J-123, J Block, Gamma 2, Greater Noida, Uttar Pradesh 201310. Hours are Monday to Saturday, 12:00 PM to 8:00 PM; Sunday is closed. Call +91 96678 63295 to ask about an appointment.</p>
      <h2>How can I respond when my child repeats my question?</h2>
      <p>Pause to give your child time. Look for a response in gesture, movement, speech or another form. If the question offered too many choices or came quickly, make the situation easier to understand. During snack, put two options where your child can see them and accept pointing or another clear choice. You can model a short phrase that fits, such as “More apple,” while acknowledging the choice the child has already made.</p>
      <p>If a repeated phrase seems connected with distress, first consider what would make the situation more comfortable. In a noisy room, a child who repeats a familiar line might also cover their ears or move toward the door. Offer a quieter space or a way to request a pause. Once things settle, tell the therapist what you noticed and how the child responded. There may be more than one explanation for the repetition.</p>
      <h2>What if the repeated phrase comes from a video or a song?</h2>
      <p>Notice where and when the phrase appears. A line about a train may come up during play because the child enjoys the sound, recognizes a connection with the toy or wants you to join. You can respond to the activity by rolling another train nearby and offering a brief comment. If the child moves away or continues playing independently, give them room. If they bring you the train or repeat the phrase with a gesture, follow that cue.</p>
      <p>It can help to share recurring phrases with other caregivers, along with what you have observed. Describe them as clues. A phrase that often accompanies a request at home may have a different use at school. Encourage each adult to watch the situation and check what the child communicates before acting.</p>
      <h2>What could a useful speech therapy goal look like?</h2>
      <p>Start with a situation where your child could use more options. For example, if adults frequently misunderstand a child who repeats a line when a game becomes too loud, a goal might be for the child to have an accessible way to ask for a break. The therapist and family could agree on a gesture, picture, device button or phrase and model it during ordinary play. Check whether the child can use it with different people and whether adults respond when the child uses it.</p>
      <p>Ask how progress will be reviewed. A useful change might be fewer communication breakdowns, more opportunities to make a choice or less frustration when a familiar adult is absent. If the child uses a new option during therapy but rarely uses it at home, ask whether the tool is available where they need it or whether the routine itself is too demanding. The point of the review is to decide what to adjust.</p>
      <h2>What should I take to a speech and language appointment?</h2>
      <p>Bring two or three examples you remember well. Write down the phrase, where your child may have heard it, what was happening before they said it, and what they did afterward. Include an example of a situation where communication went smoothly. Tell the therapist about the languages used at home and at school, and mention any previous reports or communication tools. A short written description is enough to begin the conversation.</p>
      <p>Ask about a <a href="/aasha/speech-language-therapy">speech and language assessment at Aasha in Greater Noida</a> if your child frequently has difficulty being understood, making choices or taking part in routines. If you notice a loss of established communication skills, raise it promptly with your child's clinician. Use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha booking</a> to ask which first visit fits your concern.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
